<http://data.silknow.org/object/ffb09bd8-b22c-3ed4-96b6-948afee0cd33>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "This long sword, <i>katana</i>, has fittings of iron and the copper-gold alloy <i>shakudo</i>, a wooden hilt diagonally wrapped in a dark silk cord with diamond openings to reveal the rayfish skin covering underneath, and small <i>menuki</i>, which are the ornamental metal pieces positioned either side of the hilt, held underneath the cord.  The fittings on both the scabbard and the hilt are of iron, gold, <i>shakudo</i> and other metals and are decorated with gold flowers and leaves.  The sword-guard, or <i>tsuba</i>, is similarly decorated with a pierced gold floral design.  The scabbard itself is in lacquered wood in a dark and light brown two-tone pattern with a rayfish and silk covered hilt.  A gold silk braid tying cord is wrapped around the top of the scabbard."@en , "Long sword (<i>katana</i>), blade signed <u>Tadahiro of Hizen</u> (born 1624) and scabbard of lacquered wood with rayfish and silk covered hilt; metal and <i>shakudo</i> fittings, Japan, ca. 1700-1850"@en , "Metal and <i>shakudo</i> fittings, wooden hilt diagonally wrapped in a dark silk cord, with diamond openings to reveal the rayfish skin covering underneath,  small <i>menuki</i> held underneath the cord.  The fittings on both the scabbard and the hilt are of iron, gold, <i>shakudo</i> and other metals and are decorated with gold flowers and leaves.  The sword-guard is similarly decorated with a pierced gold floral design."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/78975d27-8842-36b3-892b-c23de8f6961e> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/10c91fc1-7f71-343f-bd6e-7408ec936bf6> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "This long sword, <i>katana</i>, has fittings of iron and the copper-gold alloy <i>shakudo</i>, a wooden hilt diagonally wrapped in a dark silk cord with diamond openings to reveal the rayfish skin covering underneath, and small <i>menuki</i>, which are the ornamental metal pieces positioned either side of the hilt, held underneath the cord.  The fittings on both the scabbard and the hilt are of iron, gold, <i>shakudo</i> and other metals and are decorated with gold flowers and leaves.  The sword-guard, or <i>tsuba</i>, is similarly decorated with a pierced gold floral design.  The scabbard itself is in lacquered wood in a dark and light brown two-tone pattern with a rayfish and silk covered hilt.  A gold silk braid tying cord is wrapped around the top of the scabbard."@en , "Metal and <i>shakudo</i> fittings, wooden hilt diagonally wrapped in a dark silk cord, with diamond openings to reveal the rayfish skin covering underneath,  small <i>menuki</i> held underneath the cord.  The fittings on both the scabbard and the hilt are of iron, gold, <i>shakudo</i> and other metals and are decorated with gold flowers and leaves.  The sword-guard is similarly decorated with a pierced gold floral design."@en , "Long sword (<i>katana</i>), blade signed <u>Tadahiro of Hizen</u> (born 1624) and scabbard of lacquered wood with rayfish and silk covered hilt; metal and <i>shakudo</i> fittings, Japan, ca. 1700-1850"@en ;
